00:38:29.300 --> 00:38:32.660
And my general advice is to look for

790
00:38:32.660 --> 00:38:35.100
a plant-based milk that has

791
00:38:35.100 --> 00:38:40.100
at least 100 milligrams of calcium per 150 mils.

792
00:38:41.660 --> 00:38:44.440
And what that means is when you have a cup

793
00:38:44.440 --> 00:38:47.710
or a cup and a half, you'll be getting three,

794
00:38:47.710 --> 00:38:50.870
400 milligrams of calcium.

795
00:38:50.870 --> 00:38:53.430
And by the time you've had all of your fruits

796
00:38:53.430 --> 00:38:56.240
and vegetables, and legumes, and nuts and seeds,

797
00:38:56.240 --> 00:38:58.820
you are well above the 700 milligram

798
00:38:58.820 --> 00:39:00.783
sort of threshold where we want to be.

799
00:39:01.900 --> 00:39:06.750
But as I kind of alluded to, this is a team game,

800
00:39:06.750 --> 00:39:10.700
and you can have as much calcium in your diet as you want,

801
00:39:10.700 --> 00:39:13.560
and still develop osteoporosis.

802
00:39:13.560 --> 00:39:16.200
And we know that because countries that drink

803
00:39:16.200 --> 00:39:19.740
the most milk have the highest rates of osteoporosis.

804
00:39:19.740 --> 00:39:20.573
So--

805
00:39:20.573 --> 00:39:22.910
What is that attributable to?

806
00:39:22.910 --> 00:39:26.690
So it speaks to the fact that it is a team game,

807
00:39:26.690 --> 00:39:30.330
and there, we do see that countries

808
00:39:30.330 --> 00:39:32.180
that are consuming the most dairy

809
00:39:32.180 --> 00:39:35.300
also have the lowest vitamin D status,

810
00:39:35.300 --> 00:39:38.750
will tend to, and vitamin D is crucial

811
00:39:38.750 --> 00:39:42.230
for bone mineral density, because it actually enhances

812
00:39:42.230 --> 00:39:44.600
the absorption of calcium in your diet.

813
00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:46.620
So if you're deficient in vitamin D,

814
00:39:46.620 --> 00:39:48.273
you will absorb less calcium.

815
00:39:49.690 --> 00:39:51.810
So building strong bones,

816
00:39:51.810 --> 00:39:54.280
you need to make sure you're a cross vitamin D.

817
00:39:54.280 --> 00:39:56.910
You are hitting this calcium threshold hold,

818
00:39:56.910 --> 00:39:58.700
but then also vitamin B12,

819
00:39:58.700 --> 00:40:02.500
which we spoke about, integral, and protein.

820
00:40:02.500 --> 00:40:06.120
And if you're covering all of those bases

821
00:40:06.120 --> 00:40:11.060
and you are doing the exercise, then that is all going

822
00:40:11.060 --> 00:40:14.950
to sort of coalesce to really reduce your risk

823
00:40:14.950 --> 00:40:18.590
of developing quote unquote, "weak bones."

824
00:40:18.590 --> 00:40:22.160
So load-bearing exercise, vitamin D,

825
00:40:22.160 --> 00:40:25.173
and calcium all work in tandem.

826
00:40:26.200 --> 00:40:31.200
And I mentioned protein, and this is partly why

827
00:40:32.100 --> 00:40:35.380
the recommendations for protein as someone gets

828
00:40:35.380 --> 00:40:38.585
to sort of 50, 60 goes up a little bit,

829
00:40:38.585 --> 00:40:43.585
and this is mostly to do with trying to slow down

830
00:40:44.860 --> 00:40:47.740
this rate of bone mineral density loss,

831
00:40:47.740 --> 00:40:50.793
but also to slow down the rate of muscle loss.

832
00:40:51.660 --> 00:40:55.640
And I'm sure we might speak in a minute about protein

833
00:40:55.640 --> 00:40:56.780
and what that looks like.

834
00:40:56.780 --> 00:40:58.130
Yeah, I wanna dive into protein,

835
00:40:58.130 --> 00:40:59.670
but I wanna put a pin in that.

836
00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:01.800
Also, I have questions about plant-based milks,

837
00:41:01.800 --> 00:41:02.900
we'll table that for now,

838
00:41:02.900 --> 00:41:05.690
'cause I wanna work through these eight nutrients,

839
00:41:05.690 --> 00:41:08.510
the next being iron.

840
00:41:08.510 --> 00:41:10.710
And correct me if I'm wrong,

841
00:41:10.710 --> 00:41:15.220
but iron works similarly in that it's absorption is impacted

842
00:41:15.220 --> 00:41:18.760
by your vitamin C levels, or taking iron

843
00:41:18.760 --> 00:41:21.290
with vitamin C enhances that absorption.

844
00:41:21.290 --> 00:41:23.700
But maybe before even addressing that,

845
00:41:23.700 --> 00:41:25.660
talk a little bit about the differences

846
00:41:25.660 --> 00:41:27.373
between plant-based iron,

847
00:41:29.110 --> 00:41:30.530
in other words, non-heme iron,

848
00:41:30.530 --> 00:41:32.936
versus animal-based iron, heme iron.

849
00:41:32.936 --> 00:41:37.936
Sure, so there are two different types of iron.

850
00:41:38.530 --> 00:41:42.091
Heme iron is the type of iron you'll find in blood,

851
00:41:42.091 --> 00:41:44.710
and that's why you get it from animal foods.

852
00:41:44.710 --> 00:41:47.480
Animal foods also contained non-heme iron as well.

853
00:41:47.480 --> 00:41:50.430
About 40% of the iron in animal foods is heme

854
00:41:50.430 --> 00:41:53.470
and 60% is non-heme.

855
00:41:53.470 --> 00:41:57.770
And in plants, 100% of the ion is non-heme.

856
00:41:57.770 --> 00:42:01.930
Now broadly speaking, to keep this nice and simple,

857
00:42:01.930 --> 00:42:06.300
the main difference is that heme iron is absorbed

858
00:42:06.300 --> 00:42:09.250
more rapidly than non-heme iron.

859
00:42:09.250 --> 00:42:12.089
And that is because non-heme iron

860
00:42:12.089 --> 00:42:14.653
is often bound to other molecules,

861
00:42:16.100 --> 00:42:20.270
which can slow down and reduce the total absorption.

862
00:42:20.270 --> 00:42:22.910
But that's not necessarily a bad thing.

863
00:42:22.910 --> 00:42:25.540
There is a protective effect of that as well,

864
00:42:25.540 --> 00:42:30.130
because we know that high iron in the body,

865
00:42:30.130 --> 00:42:31.280
and particularly heme iron,

866
00:42:31.280 --> 00:42:33.270
is associated with colorectal cancer

867
00:42:33.270 --> 00:42:34.550
and cardiovascular disease.

868
00:42:34.550 --> 00:42:39.090
So this is, often, I see on social media,

869
00:42:39.090 --> 00:42:40.797
this discussion around well,

870
00:42:40.797 --> 00:42:43.650
heme iron is absorbed more rapidly,

871
00:42:43.650 --> 00:42:45.010
therefore, it must be better.

872
00:42:45.010 --> 00:42:48.220
And I think we just need to acknowledge

873
00:42:48.220 --> 00:42:50.263
that more is not always better.

874
00:42:51.390 --> 00:42:55.720
That's not to say that people eating a plant-based diet

875
00:42:55.720 --> 00:42:59.420
don't need to think about iron.

876
00:42:59.420 --> 00:43:02.220
It's the number one deficiency in the world,

877
00:43:02.220 --> 00:43:05.400
regardless of what diet people are eating.

878
00:43:05.400 --> 00:43:07.260
So this is a real issue.

879
00:43:07.260 --> 00:43:10.100
And so there will always be people that are eating

880
00:43:10.100 --> 00:43:12.650
a plant-based diet that have problems with iron.

881
00:43:12.650 --> 00:43:14.230
It's just part of being human.

882
00:43:14.230 --> 00:43:15.063
Everyone.

883
00:43:16.010 --> 00:43:18.810
Well, not everyone, but a large percentage of the population

884
00:43:18.810 --> 00:43:19.643
are dealing with this.

885
00:43:19.643 --> 00:43:22.290
Even people that are eating meat and dairy products

886
00:43:22.290 --> 00:43:24.700
three times a day are having problems with iron?

887
00:43:24.700 --> 00:43:26.190
There are absolutely still people.

888
00:43:26.190 --> 00:43:28.340
Is there a genetic predisposition

889
00:43:29.210 --> 00:43:33.510
that is problematic with respect to iron absorption?

890
00:43:33.510 --> 00:43:35.670
Or what does that cause by if somebody is eating

891
00:43:35.670 --> 00:43:37.630
plenty of foods that have iron in them?

892
00:43:37.630 --> 00:43:42.580
They could be, but also, you're at higher risk

893
00:43:42.580 --> 00:43:45.670
when you are a female of childbearing age,

894
00:43:45.670 --> 00:43:48.510
and menstruation and losing blood.

895
00:43:48.510 --> 00:43:53.230
And so, it's not all boiling down

896
00:43:53.230 --> 00:43:54.900
to just how much you consume.

897
00:43:54.900 --> 00:43:57.890
And I think that's important to note.

898
00:43:57.890 --> 00:44:01.450
There are at-risk groups, and females of childbearing age

899
00:44:01.450 --> 00:44:04.110
are right up there with the most at-risk.

900
00:44:04.110 --> 00:44:05.940
Is there a genetic component, perhaps,

901
00:44:05.940 --> 00:44:08.420
I'm not, I haven't read anything that speaks to that,

902
00:44:08.420 --> 00:44:10.330
but I wouldn't rule that out.

903
00:44:10.330 --> 00:44:14.960
I mean, a lot of omnivores are dealing with iron deficiency.

904
00:44:14.960 --> 00:44:18.810
And it's also not uncommon for someone

905
00:44:18.810 --> 00:44:20.600
from an omnivorous diet to change

906
00:44:20.600 --> 00:44:23.920
to a plant-based diet and see their iron status go up.

907
00:44:23.920 --> 00:44:25.093
That can happen.

908
00:44:26.970 --> 00:44:30.680
If you, if we look at the population studies, again,

909
00:44:30.680 --> 00:44:33.350
just comparing omnivores to vegetarians,

910
00:44:33.350 --> 00:44:36.780
there is no difference in rates of iron deficiency.

911
00:44:36.780 --> 00:44:41.780
However, vegetarians and vegans do have lower iron stores.

912
00:44:42.100 --> 00:44:44.300
And you will see that when you do your blood tests

913
00:44:44.300 --> 00:44:46.870
and one of the tests looks at iron stores.

914
00:44:46.870 --> 00:44:49.310
So not the iron, the circulating iron,

915
00:44:49.310 --> 00:44:51.050
but how much your body is storing.

916
00:44:51.050 --> 00:44:54.720
And that is typically lower in plant-based eaters,

917
00:44:54.720 --> 00:44:56.840
but is not thought to be a problem as long

918
00:44:56.840 --> 00:44:59.290
as you're topping up your iron through your diet.

919
00:45:02.210 --> 00:45:07.210
I usually like to speak to ways to enhance absorption.

920
00:45:09.200 --> 00:45:13.650
A lot of people listening to this, particularly males,

921
00:45:13.650 --> 00:45:16.320
their iron status will be completely fine,

922
00:45:16.320 --> 00:45:18.600
and they don't really need to focus on this information

923
00:45:18.600 --> 00:45:22.070
I'm about to share, because it is over-complicating things.

924
00:45:22.070 --> 00:45:25.160
So this is more for someone who potentially

925
00:45:25.160 --> 00:45:28.033
has lower iron status and is trying to bump it up a bit.

926
00:45:29.821 --> 00:45:34.821
The foods that are very rich in iron are your legumes,

927
00:45:36.730 --> 00:45:40.780
foods like pumpkin seeds, dark leafy greens.

928
00:45:40.780 --> 00:45:43.720
Chlorella is a great sort of superfood

929
00:45:43.720 --> 00:45:45.593
that's super rich in iron.

930
00:45:47.210 --> 00:45:50.620
And what we wanna focus on is regularly

931
00:45:50.620 --> 00:45:53.630
including these iron-rich foods in the diet,

932
00:45:53.630 --> 00:45:55.470
but pairing them with specific foods

933
00:45:55.470 --> 00:45:58.470
that will enhance absorption and trying to avoid

934
00:45:58.470 --> 00:46:02.413
some foods and drinks that can in inhibit absorption.

935
00:46:03.290 --> 00:46:07.410
So firstly, talking about the inhibitors, coffee and tea.

936
00:46:07.410 --> 00:46:08.710
Yeah, caffeine.

937
00:46:08.710 --> 00:46:10.060
They're the two big ones.

938
00:46:11.020 --> 00:46:14.510
They can decrease the amount of iron you are absorbing.

939
00:46:14.510 --> 00:46:18.510
So if you are sitting down for a big meal

940
00:46:18.510 --> 00:46:21.700
with lots of beans and dark leafy greens,

941
00:46:21.700 --> 00:46:24.890
it might be a good idea to separate that coffee out

942
00:46:24.890 --> 00:46:28.030
at least an hour or so, either side of the meal,

943
00:46:28.030 --> 00:46:31.680
and you will absorb more iron from that meal.

944
00:46:31.680 --> 00:46:36.130
Then there are a few things is you can add to your meals

945
00:46:36.130 --> 00:46:38.300
to enhanced absorption.

946
00:46:38.300 --> 00:46:39.900
The first one and the one that is

947
00:46:39.900 --> 00:46:44.900
the most effective, is sources of vitamin C.

948
00:46:45.030 --> 00:46:48.240
And typically, one that I recommend

949
00:46:48.240 --> 00:46:50.670
for people is lemon juice,

950
00:46:50.670 --> 00:46:52.580
squeezing that over your dark leaf greens

951
00:46:52.580 --> 00:46:55.200
or your stir fry with beans.

952
00:46:55.200 --> 00:46:57.450
But equally, there are other foods like strawberries

953
00:46:57.450 --> 00:47:00.480
that are packed with vitamin C.

954
00:47:00.480 --> 00:47:03.930
So a snack could be dried apricots,

955
00:47:03.930 --> 00:47:06.590
which are super rich in iron, with some strawberries,

956
00:47:06.590 --> 00:47:09.653
and that's a nice match.

957
00:47:11.208 --> 00:47:15.090
Bell pepper, or capsicum, we call it in Australia.

958
00:47:15.090 --> 00:47:18.740
That's another very vitamin C rich food.

959
00:47:18.740 --> 00:47:22.810
So matching these foods in your meals

960
00:47:22.810 --> 00:47:24.290
to enhance iron absorption.

961
00:47:24.290 --> 00:47:26.930
And then the other one that I think's really neat,

962
00:47:26.930 --> 00:47:29.270
and there's some cool science looking at this,

963
00:47:29.270 --> 00:47:33.060
is onion and garlic will both

964
00:47:33.060 --> 00:47:35.040
increase iron absorption significantly.

965
00:47:35.040 --> 00:47:35.893
Oh, I didn't know that one.

966
00:47:35.893 --> 00:47:40.893
So, I usually start any sort of meal, savory meal,

967
00:47:41.610 --> 00:47:44.830
stir fry, or tofu scramble in the pan

968
00:47:44.830 --> 00:47:46.110
with some onion and garlic.

969
00:47:46.110 --> 00:47:48.370
And I think a lot of people do that,

970
00:47:48.370 --> 00:47:50.730
that can be a great strategy as well

971
00:47:50.730 --> 00:47:52.830
to increase the iron absorption.

972
00:47:52.830 --> 00:47:56.050
One of the habits that I adopted a while ago

973
00:47:56.050 --> 00:48:00.850
that I still do today is I keep a bag of pumpkin seeds

974
00:48:00.850 --> 00:48:04.690
in my car with some fruit that's high in vitamin C.

975
00:48:04.690 --> 00:48:06.070
So when I'm driving around, like,

976
00:48:06.070 --> 00:48:08.260
that's like my little go-to snack

977
00:48:08.260 --> 00:48:12.060
and helps me kind of keep things topped off.

978
00:48:12.060 --> 00:48:13.260
You're on the program.

979
00:48:15.499 --> 00:48:20.370
Another habit that I've adopted is eating

980
00:48:20.370 --> 00:48:23.480
a few Brazil nuts every day or every other day,

981
00:48:23.480 --> 00:48:25.730
which are high in selenium, right?

982
00:48:25.730 --> 00:48:29.350
Which is another nutrient on your list.

983
00:48:29.350 --> 00:48:30.690
Why is selenium important?

984
00:48:30.690 --> 00:48:32.910
So selenium feeds back to thyroid, again,

985
00:48:32.910 --> 00:48:36.078
important for production of thyroid hormones.

986
00:48:36.078 --> 00:48:41.078
And as you say, it doesn't require a lot of planning

987
00:48:41.206 --> 00:48:45.840
to hit the selenium recommended intake levels,

988
00:48:45.840 --> 00:48:49.850
which are only about 70, 60, 70 micrograms a day,

989
00:48:49.850 --> 00:48:53.600
not much, one, two Brazil nuts will get you there.

990
00:48:53.600 --> 00:48:55.960
And you're also getting quite a lot of selenium

991
00:48:55.960 --> 00:48:57.050
through wheat.

992
00:48:57.050 --> 00:49:00.393
So whole wheat pasta or bread,

993
00:49:01.890 --> 00:49:04.170
and a lot of other foods like mushrooms.

994
00:49:04.170 --> 00:49:07.300
So you will be getting it elsewhere, elsewhere in your diet,

995
00:49:07.300 --> 00:49:09.123
but the addition of one or two Brazil nuts

996
00:49:09.123 --> 00:49:12.010
will just ensure you're categorically

997
00:49:12.010 --> 00:49:13.390
above that recommended level.

998
00:49:13.390 --> 00:49:15.570
And there's something about the Brazil nut

999
00:49:15.570 --> 00:49:17.620
or the selenium content of the Brazil nut

1000
00:49:17.620 --> 00:49:20.770
that has some downstream impact

1001
00:49:20.770 --> 00:49:22.961
on testosterone production, right, for dudes?

1002
00:49:22.961 --> 00:49:24.160
I haven't read that.

1003
00:49:24.160 --> 00:49:25.370
Oh, you haven't.
That's interesting.

1004
00:49:25.370 --> 00:49:28.770
Yeah, I think there's, well,

1005
00:49:28.770 --> 00:49:30.720
I don't wanna speak outta school 'cause I don't know,

1006
00:49:30.720 --> 00:49:33.230
but I'm remembering something about that.

1007
00:49:33.230 --> 00:49:34.063
Well, we can look into it.

1008
00:49:34.063 --> 00:49:35.090
Yeah, maybe you can look into that.

1009
00:49:35.090 --> 00:49:38.460
We'll link something up in the shouts about that.

1010
00:49:38.460 --> 00:49:40.760
All right, so selenium is important.

1011
00:49:40.760 --> 00:49:41.660
Why?

1012
00:49:41.660 --> 00:49:43.420
So back to thyroid it's--

1013
00:49:43.420 --> 00:49:44.377
Oh yeah, you already mentioned that.

1014
00:49:44.377 --> 00:49:45.900
And metabolism, I mean,

1015
00:49:45.900 --> 00:49:47.610
it has a whole lot of different functions

1016
00:49:47.610 --> 00:49:50.760
in the body, but the, it is integral again,

1017
00:49:50.760 --> 00:49:55.180
to thyroid health, which is integral to your metabolism

1018
00:49:56.150 --> 00:49:58.740
and management of your body weight.

1019
00:49:58.740 --> 00:50:02.730
So, I don't think we really need

1020
00:50:02.730 --> 00:50:05.880
to go into too much more than that in terms of

1021
00:50:05.880 --> 00:50:07.770
the biological sort of pathways,

1022
00:50:07.770 --> 00:50:11.317
but the main point being it's super easy

1023
00:50:11.317 --> 00:50:13.320
to get in your diet, although it's

1024
00:50:13.320 --> 00:50:17.160
in this list of eight nutrients of focus,

1025
00:50:17.160 --> 00:50:21.600
rarely would a plant-based eater not get enough selenium.

1026
00:50:21.600 --> 00:50:23.210
I just like talking about it

1027
00:50:23.210 --> 00:50:28.210
because it's a nice one to point to Brazil nuts

1028
00:50:29.830 --> 00:50:32.830
as a single food that people can look to

1029
00:50:32.830 --> 00:50:34.680
to ensure adequate intake.

1030
00:50:34.680 --> 00:50:39.680
Okay, final thing on your list of eight nutrients is zinc.

1031
00:50:40.000 --> 00:50:41.960
We're hearing a lot about zinc lately.

1032
00:50:41.960 --> 00:50:44.120
We're being told that we should be making sure

1033
00:50:44.120 --> 00:50:48.380
that we're on top of our zinc for COVID purposes.

1034
00:50:48.380 --> 00:50:51.330
So I assume without knowing, that there's some link

1035
00:50:51.330 --> 00:50:53.910
between zinc and immune system function.

1036
00:50:53.910 --> 00:50:57.840
Definitely, very important for immunity, for hair,

1037
00:50:57.840 --> 00:51:02.533
skin, nail health, for energy production,

1038
00:51:04.250 --> 00:51:06.530
preventing DNA damage.

1039
00:51:06.530 --> 00:51:08.800
It's a crucial mineral,

1040
00:51:08.800 --> 00:51:13.800
and you're going to find it mostly in nuts and legumes,

1041
00:51:14.330 --> 00:51:17.500
a kind of like the, and seeds, and hemp seeds,

1042
00:51:17.500 --> 00:51:22.440
for example, packed with zinc, but cashews,

1043
00:51:22.440 --> 00:51:25.730
another great one, pretty easy to get.

1044
00:51:25.730 --> 00:51:29.240
As long as you are regularly eating nuts

1045
00:51:29.240 --> 00:51:30.630
and seeds, and legumes,

1046
00:51:30.630 --> 00:51:35.140
which anyone who is adopting a whole food plant-based diet

1047
00:51:35.140 --> 00:51:37.890
will be, you'll have no problems reaching

1048
00:51:37.890 --> 00:51:39.960
the sort of adequate intake for that.

1049
00:51:39.960 --> 00:51:41.370
Right.

1050
00:51:41.370 --> 00:51:45.090
I think that's a good place to pivot

1051
00:51:45.090 --> 00:51:47.290
to a different category here.

1052
00:51:47.290 --> 00:51:50.550
At the outset, we kind of opened this with a discussion

1053
00:51:50.550 --> 00:51:53.200
about how to optimize your plant-based diet

1054
00:51:53.200 --> 00:51:56.310
and the idea that just because you're eating vegan,

1055
00:51:56.310 --> 00:51:58.460
doesn't necessarily mean that it's healthy,

1056
00:51:58.460 --> 00:52:02.480
there's all kinds of meat and dairy analogs out there.

1057
00:52:02.480 --> 00:52:07.000
Some of them moderately healthy, some very much not healthy.

1058
00:52:07.000 --> 00:52:09.290
So on that note,

1059
00:52:09.290 --> 00:52:12.800
let's talk a little bit about these analogs.

1060
00:52:12.800 --> 00:52:15.700
When we think about what's available now,

1061
00:52:15.700 --> 00:52:18.610
the Beyond Meats and the Impossibles,

1062
00:52:18.610 --> 00:52:19.960
are these products healthy?

1063
00:52:19.960 --> 00:52:24.960
Do they have a place in a healthy, optimal plant-based diet?

1064
00:52:26.750 --> 00:52:28.703
I think they certainly have a place,

1065
00:52:29.820 --> 00:52:33.341
no word about it,

1066
00:52:33.341 --> 00:52:36.710
but there are some that are healthier than others.

1067
00:52:36.710 --> 00:52:40.270
So it's nice to be able to be able to read the label

1068
00:52:40.270 --> 00:52:44.290
and quickly scan to see a few different things

1069
00:52:44.290 --> 00:52:48.140
and make a healthier decision.

1070
00:52:48.140 --> 00:52:50.200
And what are the things that we should be looking at

1071
00:52:50.200 --> 00:52:51.560
when we look at that label?

1072
00:52:51.560 --> 00:52:56.560
So I usually focus on sodium, on fiber and saturated fat,

1073
00:52:56.760 --> 00:53:01.250
and I'm looking for a product that has 400 milligrams

1074
00:53:01.250 --> 00:53:02.683
or less sodium per serve,

1075
00:53:03.740 --> 00:53:07.890
ideally around five grams of fiber per serve.

1076
00:53:07.890 --> 00:53:10.910
That is not always achievable.

1077
00:53:10.910 --> 00:53:12.483
That narrows the field a lot.

1078
00:53:13.550 --> 00:53:15.570
Yeah, that pretty much eliminates

1079
00:53:15.570 --> 00:53:17.560
almost everything in the category.

1080
00:53:17.560 --> 00:53:19.710
Yeah, well, not quite, but it will,

1081
00:53:19.710 --> 00:53:22.450
it'll really point you in the right direction.

1082
00:53:22.450 --> 00:53:25.200
And then saturated fat.

1083
00:53:25.200 --> 00:53:28.420
Look, this really depends on the person.

1084
00:53:28.420 --> 00:53:31.850
If you are someone who has healthy cholesterol levels,

1085
00:53:31.850 --> 00:53:35.330
and we kind of spoke about what optimal cholesterol levels

1086
00:53:35.330 --> 00:53:37.410
are previously, which is South

1087
00:53:37.410 --> 00:53:39.730
of 100 milligrams per deciliter.

1088
00:53:39.730 --> 00:53:41.540
And as you get down to 60 to 70,

1089
00:53:41.540 --> 00:53:44.230
that's where you see no atherosclerosis.

1090
00:53:44.230 --> 00:53:47.850
If you are sitting down at that sort of level,

1091
00:53:47.850 --> 00:53:49.693
you can afford to have a bit more saturated fat

1092
00:53:49.693 --> 00:53:51.600
in your diet.

1093
00:53:51.600 --> 00:53:54.490
And so when I'm working with people,

1094
00:53:54.490 --> 00:53:59.490
I encourage someone who has higher cholesterol

1095
00:53:59.690 --> 00:54:02.830
to be looking for the lower saturated fat options.

1096
00:54:02.830 --> 00:54:04.880
These are the ones that have two grams

1097
00:54:04.880 --> 00:54:08.070
or less saturated fat per serve.

1098
00:54:08.070 --> 00:54:11.890
But if your cholesterol is at an optimal level,

1099
00:54:11.890 --> 00:54:15.060
then I really see no problem with the four

1100
00:54:15.060 --> 00:54:17.820
or five grams of saturated fat per serve

1101
00:54:17.820 --> 00:54:22.120
within the context of a healthy plant-based dietary pattern.

1102
00:54:22.120 --> 00:54:26.970
And that seems to be pretty typical, a lot of them sit

1103
00:54:26.970 --> 00:54:30.530
at around three grams of saturated fat, four or five.

1104
00:54:30.530 --> 00:54:32.123
I certainly wouldn't be,

1105
00:54:33.440 --> 00:54:35.020
routinely selecting the ones

1106
00:54:35.020 --> 00:54:38.384
that have North of five grams of saturated fat per serve.

1107
00:54:38.384 --> 00:54:41.890
What about the binders and the emulsifiers,

1108
00:54:41.890 --> 00:54:44.720
and all these other ingredients that go into

1109
00:54:44.720 --> 00:54:47.960
creating that texture that people like?

1110
00:54:47.960 --> 00:54:52.960
So my answer to that would be, we need more data.

1111
00:54:53.607 --> 00:54:56.610
And I'm not saying that these are necessarily bad,

1112
00:54:56.610 --> 00:54:57.680
or they're not.

1113
00:54:57.680 --> 00:54:59.590
There just, hasn't been a lot of studies

1114
00:54:59.590 --> 00:55:01.590
on some of this, these ingredients.

1115
00:55:01.590 --> 00:55:06.550
And, there was a study that came out last week

1116
00:55:06.550 --> 00:55:11.550
on carboxymethyl cellulose, which is a common emulsifier.

1117
00:55:12.130 --> 00:55:15.830
And I spoke to Tim Specter, who's a professor

1118
00:55:15.830 --> 00:55:19.000
from King's College about this, and it was

1119
00:55:19.000 --> 00:55:23.880
a short term study, but these studies should say, Rich,

1120
00:55:23.880 --> 00:55:26.100
they're just starting to surface, looking at

1121
00:55:26.100 --> 00:55:29.180
how these ingredients interact with our microbiome,

1122
00:55:29.180 --> 00:55:30.560
for example.

1123
00:55:30.560 --> 00:55:35.560
And they did see disruption in this study to the microbiome,

1124
00:55:39.090 --> 00:55:41.680
with the consumption of this emulsifier.

1125
00:55:41.680 --> 00:55:44.650
However, it was 15 grams a day.

1126
00:55:44.650 --> 00:55:48.070
And if you are consuming one plant-based burger

1127
00:55:48.070 --> 00:55:52.990
that has, probably half a gram, a bit maximum in there,

1128
00:55:52.990 --> 00:55:56.610
then perhaps it doesn't have the same effect.

1129
00:55:56.610 --> 00:56:00.323
So in the absence of the data,

1130
00:56:01.510 --> 00:56:03.930
I'm kind of reserving my judgment

1131
00:56:03.930 --> 00:56:05.843
on a lot of those ingredients.

1132
00:56:06.760 --> 00:56:11.760
What I will say is there has been a study

1133
00:56:11.930 --> 00:56:15.730
outta Stanford University called the "SWAP-MEAT Trial."

1134
00:56:15.730 --> 00:56:17.780
And I think this is quite instructive

1135
00:56:17.780 --> 00:56:22.780
because it really, it takes a very well-known

1136
00:56:23.540 --> 00:56:27.820
plant-based meat product, Beyond Burger,

1137
00:56:27.820 --> 00:56:32.160
and stacks it up directly against meat products.

1138
00:56:32.160 --> 00:56:34.790
And they chose high-quality organic meat products

1139
00:56:34.790 --> 00:56:38.490
to make sure that this was a good study, fair.

1140
00:56:38.490 --> 00:56:42.760
And I think this is instructive because it looked more

1141
00:56:42.760 --> 00:56:46.830
at our overall risk factors for cardiovascular disease,

1142
00:56:46.830 --> 00:56:48.990
which is the leading cause of death.

1143
00:56:48.990 --> 00:56:52.130
Having a heart attack is the most likely reason

1144
00:56:52.130 --> 00:56:56.120
that you or I are to die, that's just fact.

1145
00:56:56.120 --> 00:57:01.113
And they had these subjects come in and do,

1146
00:57:02.060 --> 00:57:06.630
an eight-week randomized controlled trial crossover.

1147
00:57:06.630 --> 00:57:09.633
So you had a period where you ate the animal,

1148
00:57:10.570 --> 00:57:12.150
the animal meat products,

1149
00:57:12.150 --> 00:57:14.080
and then you had a period where you ate

1150
00:57:14.080 --> 00:57:16.200
the plant-based meats supplied by Beyond.

1151
00:57:16.200 --> 00:57:19.030
So every participant did both.

1152
00:57:19.030 --> 00:57:20.260
Every participant did both

1153
00:57:20.260 --> 00:57:23.220
and they did them in different orders.

1154
00:57:23.220 --> 00:57:28.220
And they were most interested in blood pressure,

1155
00:57:28.310 --> 00:57:32.410
LDL cholesterol, and also a biomarker

1156
00:57:32.410 --> 00:57:34.713
of cardiovascular disease called TMAO.

1157
00:57:35.980 --> 00:57:40.980
And they instructed participants to consume two serves

1158
00:57:42.550 --> 00:57:44.390
of these meat products per day.

1159
00:57:44.390 --> 00:57:47.850
So that was the kind of volume that they were exposed to.

1160
00:57:47.850 --> 00:57:52.060
And they did see over the course of this study,

1161
00:57:52.060 --> 00:57:55.230
that when subjects were consuming the plant-based meats,

1162
00:57:55.230 --> 00:57:58.040
they had significant reductions in TMAO,

1163
00:57:58.040 --> 00:58:01.360
they had significant reductions in LDL cholesterol.

1164
00:58:01.360 --> 00:58:04.250
And when, I wanna remind people,

1165
00:58:04.250 --> 00:58:05.640
this is not comparing against

1166
00:58:05.640 --> 00:58:07.220
the unhealthiest meats out there,

1167
00:58:07.220 --> 00:58:11.050
it was literally from an organic butcher in San Francisco,

1168
00:58:11.050 --> 00:58:15.770
and these were 80% lean beef products.

1169
00:58:15.770 --> 00:58:18.363
So it wasn't set up to make the beef fail.

1170
00:58:19.600 --> 00:58:22.450
And then blood pressure, they saw no significant difference.

1171
00:58:22.450 --> 00:58:25.090
And that was important because

1172
00:58:25.090 --> 00:58:28.240
a lot of these plant-based meat products

1173
00:58:28.240 --> 00:58:31.130
are often very rich in sodium, which I mentioned before.

1174
00:58:31.130 --> 00:58:36.116
So, I mean, the findings from that study are promising,

1175
00:58:36.116 --> 00:58:39.573
it's one study, it's early days,

1176
00:58:40.570 --> 00:58:44.300
but based on the nutritional profile of these products,

1177
00:58:44.300 --> 00:58:46.690
particularly if you're selecting them according

1178
00:58:46.690 --> 00:58:48.610
to how I kind of walk through,

1179
00:58:48.610 --> 00:58:50.880
and the results of that study,

1180
00:58:50.880 --> 00:58:53.210
I have no problem with these featuring within

1181
00:58:53.210 --> 00:58:56.630
a whole food plant-based dietary pattern.

1182
00:58:56.630 --> 00:58:59.380
I certainly don't think they should be the main stake

1183
00:58:59.380 --> 00:59:01.350
every single meal, because then they're coming

1184
00:59:01.350 --> 00:59:05.190
at the expense of legumes and tempeh,

1185
00:59:05.190 --> 00:59:08.160
and you beans, lentils, tempeh, tofu,

1186
00:59:08.160 --> 00:59:10.800
foods that we know absolutely are great

1187
00:59:10.800 --> 00:59:12.500
for driving down disease risk.

1188
00:59:12.500 --> 00:59:15.210
Yeah, you're gonna get no argument from me that

1189
00:59:16.600 --> 00:59:21.600
the program really should be pre-dominated by whole foods.

1190
00:59:21.810 --> 00:59:24.700
Plant-based, close to their natural state.

1191
00:59:24.700 --> 00:59:26.610
The question was really like,

1192
00:59:26.610 --> 00:59:28.590
if you have one of these every once in a while,

1193
00:59:28.590 --> 00:59:30.010
what is the impact of that?

1194
00:59:30.010 --> 00:59:33.610
And I think one of the reasons that I bring it up is there's

1195
00:59:33.610 --> 00:59:38.610
a narrative, particularly amongst sort of the paleo crowd,

1196
00:59:38.960 --> 00:59:40.860
like these are all science experiments.

1197
00:59:40.860 --> 00:59:42.340
They're terrible.

1198
00:59:42.340 --> 00:59:44.337
There are GMOs in a lot of these products,

1199
00:59:44.337 --> 00:59:48.080
and there are these emulsifiers and all these other things,

1200
00:59:48.080 --> 00:59:51.340
these binders that go into creating these products.

1201
00:59:51.340 --> 00:59:55.080
So yes, a whole food is going to always be better,

1202
00:59:55.080 --> 00:59:58.010
but how bad are they truly, like,

1203
00:59:58.010 --> 01:00:03.010
what is a trumped up narrative versus the truth?

1204
01:00:03.730 --> 01:00:05.920
And what I gather from what you're saying is we're just

1205
01:00:05.920 --> 01:00:07.950
at the beginning of studying this,

1206
01:00:07.950 --> 01:00:11.360
the Stanford University study seems to be interesting

1207
01:00:11.360 --> 01:00:13.620
in that when balanced,

1208
01:00:13.620 --> 01:00:16.930
at least against a typical burger, an organic burger,

1209
01:00:16.930 --> 01:00:18.470
it's still gonna measure out better

1210
01:00:18.470 --> 01:00:21.810
in terms of those main markers.

1211
01:00:21.810 --> 01:00:25.369
Compared to what, is like the critical question here.

1212
01:00:25.369 --> 01:00:27.990
If people are adding these into their diet

1213
01:00:27.990 --> 01:00:31.130
and it's knocking out the enormous amounts

1214
01:00:31.130 --> 01:00:34.660
of animal protein, I think it's definitely shifting

1215
01:00:34.660 --> 01:00:38.253
their overall health profile in the right direction.

1216
01:00:39.200 --> 01:00:44.200
And I'm also mindful of the fact that consistency

1217
01:00:44.440 --> 01:00:47.370
over time is far more important than

1218
01:00:47.370 --> 01:00:52.030
how healthy every single food or every single meal is.

1219
01:00:52.030 --> 01:00:55.100
And some of these foods make it easier

1220
01:00:55.100 --> 01:00:58.820
for people to sustain an otherwise whole food,

1221
01:00:58.820 --> 01:01:01.570
plant-based dietary pattern for longer.

1222
01:01:01.570 --> 01:01:05.223
Some of that can be down to social circumstances,

1223
01:01:06.310 --> 01:01:07.640
many different factors.

1224
01:01:07.640 --> 01:01:10.550
So my sort of view of them is

1225
01:01:10.550 --> 01:01:12.610
I certainly wouldn't want to vilify them,

1226
01:01:12.610 --> 01:01:15.020
I think they serve a role.

1227
01:01:15.020 --> 01:01:16.900
With that said, I would love more

1228
01:01:16.900 --> 01:01:18.840
and more studies to be done on them.

1229
01:01:18.840 --> 01:01:22.960
And I think that we should hold these companies accountable.

1230
01:01:22.960 --> 01:01:24.670
So that as more data comes out

1231
01:01:24.670 --> 01:01:28.740
and we understand more, they are reformulating,

1232
01:01:28.740 --> 01:01:32.220
and continuing to improve their product

1233
01:01:32.220 --> 01:01:34.010
to make it not just delicious

1234
01:01:34.010 --> 01:01:36.180
and a great alternative to meat, but healthier as well.

1235
01:01:36.180 --> 01:01:39.130
Yeah, particularly the more ubiquitous they become

1236
01:01:39.130 --> 01:01:42.700
and the more they find their way into kind of our fast food

1237
01:01:42.700 --> 01:01:44.890
and fast casual infrastructure,

1238
01:01:44.890 --> 01:01:46.450
because so many people are eating them.

1239
01:01:46.450 --> 01:01:47.683
So there is an accountability thing

1240
01:01:47.683 --> 01:01:49.387
that I think is important here.

1241
01:01:49.387 --> 01:01:51.980
And my hope is that as these things continue

1242
01:01:51.980 --> 01:01:55.960
to scale, that we're also seeing the on-ramping

1243
01:01:55.960 --> 01:01:57.837
of healthier versions of all of these things.

1244
01:01:57.837 --> 01:01:59.130
And I'm already seeing that.

1245
01:01:59.130 --> 01:02:01.770
Like I just was in Vancouver

1246
01:02:01.770 --> 01:02:04.430
at this big plant-based expo,

1247
01:02:04.430 --> 01:02:05.803
and there must have been 200 exhibitors there,

1248
01:02:05.803 --> 01:02:07.810
there's so many food companies

1249
01:02:07.810 --> 01:02:09.350
that are doing interesting things,

1250
01:02:09.350 --> 01:02:13.430
and to see what people are doing with mushrooms

1251
01:02:13.430 --> 01:02:16.160
and all these like ingredients that you never

1252
01:02:16.160 --> 01:02:19.600
would've thought would comprise like the architecture of

1253
01:02:19.600 --> 01:02:22.700
a plant-based food, but doing it with natural ingredients.

1254
01:02:22.700 --> 01:02:23.980
And I think that's pretty exciting.

1255
01:02:23.980 --> 01:02:24.813
Yeah, I love the,

1256
01:02:24.813 --> 01:02:27.253
there's some really great mushroom products coming out.

1257
01:02:27.253 --> 01:02:32.120
There's a few of those in Australia and I'm very optimistic.

1258
01:02:32.120 --> 01:02:36.060
For example, there is a company in Australia called BUDS.

1259
01:02:36.060 --> 01:02:40.330
And I like the fact that they're Australian,

1260
01:02:40.330 --> 01:02:42.840
because there's not a whole lot of kind of big players

1261
01:02:42.840 --> 01:02:45.640
in this space that are, so I'm kind of rooting for them.

1262
01:02:46.580 --> 01:02:50.460
But I say that because they have contacted me

1263
01:02:50.460 --> 01:02:51.990
and I've spoken to them about

1264
01:02:51.990 --> 01:02:54.010
the nutritional profile of their product.

1265
01:02:54.010 --> 01:02:58.650
So I do genuinely think that these companies are aware

1266
01:02:58.650 --> 01:03:00.770
that they need to not only be an alternative,

1267
01:03:00.770 --> 01:03:03.100
they do need to be healthier.

1268
01:03:03.100 --> 01:03:06.230
And a great example of that is back to Beyond Meat.

1269
01:03:06.230 --> 01:03:09.140
They just released, I think two new burgers.

1270
01:03:09.140 --> 01:03:13.160
One is I think about 50% reduced saturated fat,

1271
01:03:13.160 --> 01:03:15.370
and the other is 30% reduced saturated fat,

1272
01:03:15.370 --> 01:03:17.230
and these are iterations for them.

1273
01:03:17.230 --> 01:03:21.800
So, that change, that that will happen.

1274
01:03:21.800 --> 01:03:25.010
And the key message being,

1275
01:03:25.010 --> 01:03:27.850
they probably shouldn't be the absolute mainstay

1276
01:03:27.850 --> 01:03:30.730
of your diet, but their inclusion can make

1277
01:03:30.730 --> 01:03:33.430
a diet more enjoyable for certain people.

1278
01:03:33.430 --> 01:03:37.610
And let's also keep in mind that compared

1279
01:03:37.610 --> 01:03:39.040
to the products they're displacing,

1280
01:03:39.040 --> 01:03:41.230
they're much better the environment

1281
01:03:41.230 --> 01:03:43.810
from a land use point-of-view,

1282
01:03:43.810 --> 01:03:46.900
from a water preservation point-of-view,

1283
01:03:46.900 --> 01:03:49.690
from a greenhouse gas and meeting point-of-view.

1284
01:03:49.690 --> 01:03:53.670
And at the same time, they're kinder

1285
01:03:53.670 --> 01:03:55.700
to all life on the planet.

1286
01:03:55.700 --> 01:03:56.580
Yeah, 100%.

1287
01:03:56.580 --> 01:04:00.260
But it's also compared to what situation as well,

1288
01:04:00.260 --> 01:04:03.930
because most of them are not organic,

1289
01:04:03.930 --> 01:04:07.190
and they rely on monocropping for their supply chain.

1290
01:04:07.190 --> 01:04:09.570
So there's a whole other like layer

1291
01:04:09.570 --> 01:04:12.360
or level of creating these things

1292
01:04:12.360 --> 01:04:15.220
in a more sustainable healthy way.

1293
01:04:15.220 --> 01:04:17.860
I think that's part of holding them accountable.

1294
01:04:17.860 --> 01:04:18.970
Very much at the moment,

1295
01:04:18.970 --> 01:04:21.690
they kind of seem to be from my understanding

1296
01:04:21.690 --> 01:04:22.900
and speaking to certain people,

1297
01:04:22.900 --> 01:04:26.050
is that they're piggybacking off of a lot of

1298
01:04:26.050 --> 01:04:28.280
the sort of monocrops that are grown,

1299
01:04:28.280 --> 01:04:31.690
that are fed into the animal agriculture industry.

1300
01:04:31.690 --> 01:04:35.500
But what certainly, what I'd like to see going forward

1301
01:04:35.500 --> 01:04:39.350
is these companies supporting regenerative agriculture,

1302
01:04:39.350 --> 01:04:42.130
and not just using monocropped ingredients,

1303
01:04:42.130 --> 01:04:43.108
thinking about polycropping,

1304
01:04:43.108 --> 01:04:46.150
and that's just another component

1305
01:04:46.150 --> 01:04:47.960
of holding them accountable.

1306
01:04:47.960 --> 01:04:49.760
And most of them are pledging

1307
01:04:49.760 --> 01:04:53.410
that they are pro-planetary health.

1308
01:04:53.410 --> 01:04:56.600
So, I'm optimistic.

1309
01:04:56.600 --> 01:04:59.410
Well, of course, that's the whole reason

1310
01:04:59.410 --> 01:05:01.130
for their existence.

1311
01:05:01.130 --> 01:05:03.590
But I think the real value ad proposition here is

1312
01:05:03.590 --> 01:05:06.360
that it's transitioning other people

1313
01:05:06.360 --> 01:05:09.780
who are otherwise not considering going plant-based at all,

1314
01:05:09.780 --> 01:05:13.460
into a more plant-oriented diet

1315
01:05:13.460 --> 01:05:15.420
and getting them off these animal products.

1316
01:05:15.420 --> 01:05:17.760
So it's, again, it goes back to the comparison

1317
01:05:17.760 --> 01:05:20.200
against what are you comparing these things to.

1318
01:05:20.200 --> 01:05:24.800
And part of that transition period,

1319
01:05:24.800 --> 01:05:28.010
it can be difficult to acclimatize to more fiber.

1320
01:05:28.010 --> 01:05:30.560
And so that's where some of these products

1321
01:05:30.560 --> 01:05:32.630
can be really helpful.

1322
01:05:32.630 --> 01:05:35.083
And I actually recommend them to a number of people

1323
01:05:35.083 --> 01:05:39.340
that are going through the steps of trying

1324
01:05:39.340 --> 01:05:42.170
to reduce animal products in their diet.

1325
01:05:42.170 --> 01:05:45.380
And perhaps they're finding the sudden increase

1326
01:05:45.380 --> 01:05:49.203
in legumes to be a little bit like an atomic bomb.

1327
01:05:50.650 --> 01:05:53.340
And that is one way of helping back off

1328
01:05:53.340 --> 01:05:54.680
the fiber a little bit

1329
01:05:54.680 --> 01:05:57.710
and make that process more comfortable.

1330
01:05:57.710 --> 01:05:59.040
Well, let's talk about that for a minute

1331
01:05:59.040 --> 01:06:00.940
because that's another thing that I hear a lot,

1332
01:06:00.940 --> 01:06:02.670
I'm sure you do as well.

1333
01:06:02.670 --> 01:06:04.917
I tried a plant-based diet and the beans

1334
01:06:04.917 --> 01:06:06.920
and the legumes and all of that,

1335
01:06:06.920 --> 01:06:08.910
I just was so bloated and gassy,

1336
01:06:08.910 --> 01:06:10.850
and I just couldn't hang with it.

1337
01:06:10.850 --> 01:06:11.950
It's a process.

1338
01:06:11.950 --> 01:06:13.880
And we spoke about that other,

1339
01:06:13.880 --> 01:06:17.120
the fermented food fiber study last time,

1340
01:06:17.120 --> 01:06:20.600
which I think illustrated that the addition of fiber

1341
01:06:20.600 --> 01:06:23.330
to the diet will have varying effects on people

1342
01:06:23.330 --> 01:06:25.600
depending on where their baseline microbiome is.

1343
01:06:25.600 --> 01:06:29.793
So if someone has a more severely disrupted microbiome,

1344
01:06:30.640 --> 01:06:34.210
perhaps they had a lot of antibiotics growing up,

1345
01:06:34.210 --> 01:06:39.200
and have been eating a very high ultra-processed food diet

1346
01:06:39.200 --> 01:06:41.630
that we know is disruptive.

1347
01:06:41.630 --> 01:06:44.720
They may struggle a little more

1348
01:06:44.720 --> 01:06:48.420
with increasing their fiber intake very rapidly.

1349
01:06:48.420 --> 01:06:53.220
So going slow and low is from my experience

1350
01:06:53.220 --> 01:06:57.330
by far the best strategy, rather than an over the night,

1351
01:06:57.330 --> 01:06:59.750
changing from the average intake of fiber is

1352
01:06:59.750 --> 01:07:04.240
about 12 to 15 grams in Australia and America.

1353
01:07:04.240 --> 01:07:05.550
Going from that to North of 30,

1354
01:07:05.550 --> 01:07:10.160
40 grams is going to really shock your system.

1355
01:07:10.160 --> 01:07:13.880
So I usually work on ramping it up with people,

1356
01:07:13.880 --> 01:07:16.040
starting with small increments, for example,

1357
01:07:16.040 --> 01:07:18.930
a quarter of a cup of cooked lentils is

1358
01:07:18.930 --> 01:07:21.210
about out four grams of fiber.

1359
01:07:21.210 --> 01:07:22.730
And that's how much I'll get people

1360
01:07:22.730 --> 01:07:26.430
to modulate their diet by every day, second day.

1361
01:07:26.430 --> 01:07:29.160
And to step through these changes

1362
01:07:29.160 --> 01:07:31.740
over two, three, four months,

1363
01:07:31.740 --> 01:07:35.560
rather than trying to do it all in one week.

1364
01:07:35.560 --> 01:07:37.810
On the topic of legumes,

1365
01:07:37.810 --> 01:07:42.810
a game changer that I've personally felt and used,

1366
01:07:43.080 --> 01:07:45.600
and also something that I know has helped

1367
01:07:45.600 --> 01:07:47.090
a lot of other people,

1368
01:07:47.090 --> 01:07:50.080
is pressure cookers, an instant pot.

1369
01:07:50.080 --> 01:07:51.880
Have you heard of that?
Mm hm, yeah, we have one.

1370
01:07:51.880 --> 01:07:56.880
Yeah, so, I mean, if you're buying dried beans,

1371
01:07:57.310 --> 01:08:00.680
they need to be soaked and then cooked,

1372
01:08:00.680 --> 01:08:03.670
that can be a very drawn-out, long process,

1373
01:08:03.670 --> 01:08:07.323
and some people don't have the time to do that.

1374
01:08:08.470 --> 01:08:11.760
And a pressure cooker is a much faster way of doing it.

1375
01:08:11.760 --> 01:08:15.130
You still can soak them beforehand,

1376
01:08:15.130 --> 01:08:17.730
but soaking doesn't take up a lot of your own time,

1377
01:08:17.730 --> 01:08:19.840
and it is optional, but the pressure cooker

1378
01:08:19.840 --> 01:08:23.530
will essentially, you'll have your beans

1379
01:08:23.530 --> 01:08:25.280
or legumes ready in an hour,

1380
01:08:25.280 --> 01:08:27.940
hour and a half, depending on the type.

1381
01:08:27.940 --> 01:08:30.630
And certainly makes them a lot more digestible.

1382
01:08:30.630 --> 01:08:33.350
And you can add into there some ginger or kombu,

1383
01:08:33.350 --> 01:08:35.010
which is a type of seaweed,

1384
01:08:35.010 --> 01:08:38.570
which I haven't found a whole lot of good data on,

1385
01:08:38.570 --> 01:08:42.890
but anecdotally does seem to be helping people digest them.

1386
01:08:42.890 --> 01:08:45.900
And so that's one strategy

1387
01:08:45.900 --> 01:08:47.810
that I do recommend is making sure

1388
01:08:47.810 --> 01:08:50.190
you're preparing your legumes properly.

1389
01:08:50.190 --> 01:08:53.040
And in doing that, you'll reduce some of

1390
01:08:53.040 --> 01:08:56.000
the fermentable carbohydrates,

1391
01:08:56.000 --> 01:09:00.080
the sort of gas-producing carbohydrates and thus,

1392
01:09:00.080 --> 01:09:02.770
they'll be a little bit easier to tolerate.

1393
01:09:02.770 --> 01:09:04.970
If you're buying canned beans,

1394
01:09:04.970 --> 01:09:09.780
you can also reduce some of those gas-producing molecules

1395
01:09:09.780 --> 01:09:12.610
by rinsing them very thoroughly

1396
01:09:12.610 --> 01:09:15.080
under water before you eat them.

1397
01:09:15.080 --> 01:09:17.180
But ostensibly, what you're saying is

1398
01:09:17.180 --> 01:09:19.090
in terms of this acclimation period,

1399
01:09:19.090 --> 01:09:22.970
it's really a period of time in which your microbiome has

1400
01:09:22.970 --> 01:09:27.810
to adjust to this different type of food.

1401
01:09:27.810 --> 01:09:31.690
And by eating it, you're kind of seeding your gut lining

1402
01:09:31.690 --> 01:09:35.980
with the prebiotic, the biota, ostensibly, that you need

1403
01:09:35.980 --> 01:09:40.273
to create this new flora that then can digest these foods

1404
01:09:40.273 --> 01:09:42.243
without the bloating and the gassiness.

1405
01:09:43.700 --> 01:09:48.700
Yeah, and so exactly, it's a little bit like fertilizer,

1406
01:09:50.408 --> 01:09:53.270
you're sprinkling this fertilizer over

1407
01:09:53.270 --> 01:09:57.820
the microbes and they will proliferate,

1408
01:09:57.820 --> 01:09:59.860
and the microbes that are fermenting

1409
01:09:59.860 --> 01:10:02.490
these carbohydrates will grow in numbers.

1410
01:10:02.490 --> 01:10:06.790
And so your gut, put simply, will grow stronger.

1411
01:10:06.790 --> 01:10:08.380
Similar to going into the gym,

1412
01:10:08.380 --> 01:10:11.780
picking up a weight, and progressively increasing

1413
01:10:11.780 --> 01:10:14.363
that weight, your muscle will grow stronger.

1414
01:10:15.560 --> 01:10:20.560
And the downstream effect of doing that is creating

1415
01:10:20.800 --> 01:10:23.890
a more diverse microbiome, which produces more

1416
01:10:23.890 --> 01:10:27.300
of those wonderful short chain fatty acids,

1417
01:10:27.300 --> 01:10:30.900
which help maintain the integrity of that mucosal layer

1418
01:10:30.900 --> 01:10:34.080
and the epithelial cells that line

1419
01:10:34.080 --> 01:10:39.080
the large intestine, and helps reduce inflammation

1420
01:10:39.210 --> 01:10:42.220
and improve blood glucose control.

1421
01:10:42.220 --> 01:10:44.010
All those things we spoke about, previously.

1422
01:10:44.010 --> 01:10:47.860
Right, the diversity of plant foods contributes

1423
01:10:47.860 --> 01:10:52.520
to a more diverse microbiome, which makes you more robust.

1424
01:10:52.520 --> 01:10:54.340
The analogy being when you go to the gym,

1425
01:10:54.340 --> 01:10:55.225
you do all different kinds of,

1426
01:10:55.225 --> 01:10:58.190
as many different types of exercises as possible

1427
01:10:58.190 --> 01:11:02.100
to become as bulletproof as possible.

1428
01:11:02.100 --> 01:11:04.710
Yeah, and I've kind of oversimplified it

1429
01:11:04.710 --> 01:11:08.040
a little bit there, there is more going on

1430
01:11:08.040 --> 01:11:12.531
in terms of the microbiota composition.

1431
01:11:12.531 --> 01:11:15.000
We are just learning now about polyphenols,

1432
01:11:15.000 --> 01:11:16.690
how they affect the microbiome.

1433
01:11:16.690 --> 01:11:19.090
I like how excited you get.

1434
01:11:19.090 --> 01:11:20.610
Every time you say polyphenols,

1435
01:11:20.610 --> 01:11:22.210
you get this big smile on your face.

1436
01:11:22.210 --> 01:11:25.000
Like, the only person who smiles more broadly

1437
01:11:25.000 --> 01:11:27.800
with that word is Dr. B, I think.

1438
01:11:27.800 --> 01:11:29.670
Yeah, and ironically,

1439
01:11:29.670 --> 01:11:33.880
we've had some great chats about them, but,

1440
01:11:33.880 --> 01:11:35.447
I say that because like black beans for example,

1441
01:11:35.447 --> 01:11:37.520
are very rich in polyphenols.

1442
01:11:37.520 --> 01:11:40.600
And so it's not just fiber that is modulating

1443
01:11:40.600 --> 01:11:43.863
the microbiome, but also these polyphenols, which,

1444
01:11:44.730 --> 01:11:45.870
just five, 10 years ago,

1445
01:11:45.870 --> 01:11:48.880
we thought were all absorbed in the small intestine.

1446
01:11:48.880 --> 01:11:51.730
Most of them are traveling through to the large intestine

1447
01:11:51.730 --> 01:11:55.710
where they are directly affecting the microbiota,

1448
01:11:55.710 --> 01:12:00.710
where they're acting as another form of prebiotic.

1449
01:12:00.850 --> 01:12:02.490
They fall under the same banner

1450
01:12:02.490 --> 01:12:05.250
as prebiotic fiber and resistance starch.

1451
01:12:05.250 --> 01:12:09.470
And they provoke an increase in the proliferation

1452
01:12:09.470 --> 01:12:13.232
of these short chain fatty acid-producing bacteria.

1453
01:12:13.232 --> 01:12:16.830
And there was a recent study that came out,

1454
01:12:16.830 --> 01:12:20.260
which took older people.

1455
01:12:20.260 --> 01:12:23.540
These were people that were aged about 66 years of age

1456
01:12:23.540 --> 01:12:28.190
and did a randomized controlled trial, and very neat design,

1457
01:12:28.190 --> 01:12:30.770
because they held the fiber the same,

1458
01:12:30.770 --> 01:12:33.570
but just increased polyphenols in one arm.

1459
01:12:33.570 --> 01:12:37.250
And they saw that the group who added these polyphenols,

1460
01:12:37.250 --> 01:12:39.330
and that was largely from like green tea

1461
01:12:39.330 --> 01:12:42.680
and pomegranate, and some cacao,

1462
01:12:42.680 --> 01:12:45.190
these are all very polyphenol-rich foods.

1463
01:12:45.190 --> 01:12:48.840
They had decreased levels of vazaulin,

1464
01:12:48.840 --> 01:12:52.140
which is a marker of intestinal permeability, or,

1465
01:12:52.140 --> 01:12:53.677
quote unquote, "leaky gut."

1466
01:12:54.710 --> 01:12:56.540
So, you know.

1467
01:12:56.540 --> 01:12:58.180
So for somebody who suffers

1468
01:12:58.180 --> 01:13:01.140
from some kind of ulcerative colitis

1469
01:13:01.140 --> 01:13:04.330
or autoimmune gut-related disorder,

1470
01:13:04.330 --> 01:13:06.810
amping up your polyphenols might be a good idea.

1471
01:13:06.810 --> 01:13:08.650
Yeah, and the idea here,

1472
01:13:08.650 --> 01:13:11.080
and this is very, very preliminary,

1473
01:13:11.080 --> 01:13:12.850
but there are some theories out there,

1474
01:13:12.850 --> 01:13:17.730
that where someone is not tolerating the fiber,

1475
01:13:17.730 --> 01:13:20.390
the increase in fiber so much,

1476
01:13:20.390 --> 01:13:24.640
it might be a good strategy to increase polyphenols first.

1477
01:13:24.640 --> 01:13:28.110
And from some of these foods where they're incredibly rich

1478
01:13:28.110 --> 01:13:30.280
in polyphenols, but quite low in fiber,

1479
01:13:30.280 --> 01:13:32.510
like green tea, for example,

1480
01:13:32.510 --> 01:13:35.570
as a way to encourage microbiome diversity,

1481
01:13:35.570 --> 01:13:38.960
similar to what we spoke about with the fermented foods.

1482
01:13:38.960 --> 01:13:40.940
Right, so you're increasing,

1483
01:13:40.940 --> 01:13:44.200
or you're decreasing gut permeability prior

1484
01:13:44.200 --> 01:13:47.140
to sort of ramping up the fiber,

1485
01:13:47.140 --> 01:13:50.070
which might cause some distress if you have

1486
01:13:50.070 --> 01:13:51.880
a permeability issue.

1487
01:13:51.880 --> 01:13:55.280
Yeah, and that's a, that's something that an experiment,

1488
01:13:55.280 --> 01:13:56.590
no doubt will test.

1489
01:13:56.590 --> 01:13:58.410
And I think the Sonnenburgs at Stanford

1490
01:13:58.410 --> 01:14:00.010
are doing something similar.

1491
01:14:00.010 --> 01:14:03.130
So we'll know more about that, but in theory,

1492
01:14:03.130 --> 01:14:06.320
from a mechanistic point-of-view, it does make sense.

1493
01:14:06.320 --> 01:14:09.450
Yeah, I gotta get those Sonnenburgs on the podcast.

1494
01:14:09.450 --> 01:14:11.788
Have you spoken to them? We talked about that, didn't we?

1495
01:14:11.788 --> 01:14:12.660
I think we talked about last time.

1496
01:14:12.660 --> 01:14:14.916
Well, actually only spoke to Justin

1497
01:14:14.916 --> 01:14:17.740
about four days ago for the first time.

1498
01:14:17.740 --> 01:14:20.200
I spoken many times to Chris Gardner,

1499
01:14:20.200 --> 01:14:21.033
but yeah, you should get them.

1500
01:14:21.033 --> 01:14:22.080
Chris Gardner's the one who did

1501
01:14:22.080 --> 01:14:25.180
the Beyond versus organic beef study, right?

1502
01:14:25.180 --> 01:14:26.750
And he comes up with all these acronyms,

1503
01:14:26.750 --> 01:14:29.420
like the "SWAP-MEAT Trial," and like DIETFITS.

1504
01:14:29.420 --> 01:14:31.930
And he's a laugh, but yeah,

1505
01:14:31.930 --> 01:14:34.610
the Sonnenburgs, both Justin and Erica,

1506
01:14:34.610 --> 01:14:37.280
are like the wizards when it comes to the microbiome.

1507
01:14:37.280 --> 01:14:39.723
So certainly, yeah, you should get them on.

1508
01:14:42.230 --> 01:14:44.130
I hear everything you're saying,

1509
01:14:44.130 --> 01:14:48.620
but Simon, with respect to beans at least,

1510
01:14:48.620 --> 01:14:51.403
Dr. Gundry told me I gotta worry about these lectins.

1511
01:14:54.960 --> 01:14:59.960
Well, this comes back to understanding exposure level.

1512
01:15:00.880 --> 01:15:02.450
When we consider whether a food

1513
01:15:02.450 --> 01:15:05.420
or a nutrient is healthy or harmful.

1514
01:15:05.420 --> 01:15:10.420
And an analogy here that I'll use is oxygen.

1515
01:15:12.000 --> 01:15:17.000
If I gave you pure oxygen, 100% oxygen,

1516
01:15:17.840 --> 01:15:21.363
you would pass out and eventually die.

1517
01:15:22.260 --> 01:15:25.280
But I'm sure that you would agree with me

1518
01:15:25.280 --> 01:15:28.960
that oxygen is healthy and it's life sustaining,

1519
01:15:28.960 --> 01:15:29.793
and we need it.

1520
01:15:29.793 --> 01:15:31.433
It's in our air, right?

1521
01:15:32.631 --> 01:15:37.631
Oxygen in air is at 26%, I believe is the concentration.

1522
01:15:39.330 --> 01:15:41.463
And at that level, it is very healthy.

1523
01:15:42.460 --> 01:15:46.170
The dose makes the poison, and this is similar with lectins.

1524
01:15:46.170 --> 01:15:50.400
We could cherry-pick and look at some mechanistic studies,

1525
01:15:50.400 --> 01:15:52.680
animal studies, where they're exposed

1526
01:15:52.680 --> 01:15:56.170
to very, very high, extremely high levels of lectins

1527
01:15:56.170 --> 01:15:58.500
that you would never be exposed to in your diet.

1528
01:15:58.500 --> 01:16:02.560
And we could show some impairment of the microbiome

1529
01:16:02.560 --> 01:16:05.993
or the intestinal barrier and leaky gut.

1530
01:16:06.900 --> 01:16:10.740
However, if we zoom back out

1531
01:16:10.740 --> 01:16:15.620
and look at all of the data on people consuming legumes,

1532
01:16:15.620 --> 01:16:19.730
observational data shows that there are the hallmark of

1533
01:16:19.730 --> 01:16:22.323
the diets of the people who live the longest,

1534
01:16:24.844 --> 01:16:27.170
and then we see experimentally

1535
01:16:27.170 --> 01:16:29.480
in randomized controlled trials,

1536
01:16:29.480 --> 01:16:32.185
that the addition of these foods

1537
01:16:32.185 --> 01:16:34.880
help improve biomarkers of disease,

1538
01:16:34.880 --> 01:16:37.100
like cholesterol, for example.

1539
01:16:37.100 --> 01:16:40.533
So the dose really makes the poison.

1540
01:16:41.720 --> 01:16:44.670
Beans do contain lectins, that's true,

1541
01:16:44.670 --> 01:16:48.570
and it's why I wouldn't recommend eating dried beans.

1542
01:16:48.570 --> 01:16:50.630
But nobody eats dried beans.

1543
01:16:50.630 --> 01:16:53.170
Nobody does, except there is one study where,

1544
01:16:53.170 --> 01:16:57.620
and this is cited, where patients,

1545
01:16:57.620 --> 01:17:00.600
if I recall correctly, in Japan, were accidentally

1546
01:17:00.600 --> 01:17:05.600
fed raw, improperly prepared legumes,

1547
01:17:05.660 --> 01:17:08.620
and they had an acute reaction response to it.

1548
01:17:08.620 --> 01:17:11.590
And certainly I just wouldn't recommend doing it.

1549
01:17:11.590 --> 01:17:12.500
It's why we soak.

1550
01:17:12.500 --> 01:17:14.080
It's why we use a pressure cooker.

1551
01:17:14.080 --> 01:17:18.240
It reduces lectins and they're still there,

1552
01:17:18.240 --> 01:17:20.470
but they're reduced to a level where they're actually

1553
01:17:20.470 --> 01:17:22.270
thought to be very beneficial.

1554
01:17:22.270 --> 01:17:25.073
And this is the sort of ironic thing.

1555
01:17:25.073 --> 01:17:29.113
And you could run a search and look at this online.

1556
01:17:30.800 --> 01:17:33.760
There are just as many mechanistic studies

1557
01:17:33.760 --> 01:17:36.990
showing benefits of lectins and that lectins

1558
01:17:36.990 --> 01:17:40.283
actually have anticarcinogenic properties.

1559
01:17:41.500 --> 01:17:43.180
So why does Gundry have such a bee

1560
01:17:43.180 --> 01:17:45.490
in his bonnet over this?

1561
01:17:45.490 --> 01:17:47.690
Well, I think that he's over-extrapolating

1562
01:17:48.689 --> 01:17:52.870
from these studies in animals where they are exposed

1563
01:17:52.870 --> 01:17:55.400
to enormous amount of lectins.

1564
01:17:55.400 --> 01:17:58.890
I haven't seen any good evidence to suggest

1565
01:17:58.890 --> 01:18:01.920
that we should be worried about lectins

1566
01:18:01.920 --> 01:18:05.420
in properly prepared legumes.

1567
01:18:05.420 --> 01:18:08.660
And very, very consistently,

1568
01:18:08.660 --> 01:18:11.040
legumes are shown to improve health.

1569
01:18:11.040 --> 01:18:13.870
They're arguably one of the healthiest foods

1570
01:18:13.870 --> 01:18:16.280
out there on planet Earth.

1571
01:18:16.280 --> 01:18:21.280
So, it's an absolute message and it helps sell a book.

1572
01:18:23.170 --> 01:18:24.003
I'm not sure what else I can say.

1573
01:18:24.003 --> 01:18:25.810
Yeah, I mean, it is the consistent theme

1574
01:18:25.810 --> 01:18:27.760
across all the blue zones.

1575
01:18:27.760 --> 01:18:30.190
And Dan Bruner is always talking about beans

1576
01:18:30.190 --> 01:18:34.443
being really the centerpiece of a healthy diet.

1577
01:18:35.700 --> 01:18:39.623
So I think we can put to rest the lectin fear.

1578
01:18:40.640 --> 01:18:42.580
And then with that said,

1579
01:18:42.580 --> 01:18:46.380
still acknowledge that when you are adding legumes

1580
01:18:46.380 --> 01:18:49.120
to your diet and you haven't had them in your diet,

1581
01:18:49.120 --> 01:18:51.870
that it's going to take some time for your microbiome

1582
01:18:51.870 --> 01:18:54.850
to adjust, go low, go slow,

1583
01:18:54.850 --> 01:18:57.400
use some of those strategies that we spoke about,

1584
01:18:57.400 --> 01:18:59.920
and you will absolutely get there.

1585
01:18:59.920 --> 01:19:00.890
Right, you're gonna fart

1586
01:19:00.890 --> 01:19:02.430
and you're gonna burp a little bit,

1587
01:19:02.430 --> 01:19:03.310
but it'll be fine.

1588
01:19:03.310 --> 01:19:07.040
It'll be a bumpy ride, but it'll be worth it.

1589
01:19:07.040 --> 01:19:11.930
So Simon, animal protein versus plant protein.

1590
01:19:11.930 --> 01:19:14.380
Perhaps the number one concern for most people

1591
01:19:14.380 --> 01:19:16.900
who are considering a plant-based diet.

1592
01:19:16.900 --> 01:19:21.900
Hmm, let's break this down to basics first.

1593
01:19:22.730 --> 01:19:23.870
Protein, what is it?

1594
01:19:23.870 --> 01:19:25.920
It's amino acids.

1595
01:19:25.920 --> 01:19:29.017
And they're building blocks of all of

1596
01:19:29.017 --> 01:19:32.540
the 30,000-odd proteins in body.

1597
01:19:32.540 --> 01:19:37.540
And there are 11 non-essential amino acids.

1598
01:19:38.400 --> 01:19:41.120
Those are amino acids

1599
01:19:41.120 --> 01:19:43.907
our body can produce endogenously.

1600
01:19:45.846 --> 01:19:49.380
And then there are nine essential amino acids,

1601
01:19:49.380 --> 01:19:51.450
which our body cannot produce,

1602
01:19:51.450 --> 01:19:53.830
and therefore, it's critical that we are getting them

1603
01:19:53.830 --> 01:19:57.230
in our diet in some way, shape, or form,

1604
01:19:57.230 --> 01:19:58.763
in the required amounts.

1605
01:20:00.140 --> 01:20:04.980
We covered previously, that there is a myth

1606
01:20:04.980 --> 01:20:09.980
that plant-based foods are missing essential amino acids.

1607
01:20:10.230 --> 01:20:12.430
And that's not true.

1608
01:20:12.430 --> 01:20:17.430
All plants do contain all nine essential amino acids.

1609
01:20:17.580 --> 01:20:22.540
So it's not as if when you are removing animal foods

1610
01:20:22.540 --> 01:20:24.260
and you're going to plant foods

1611
01:20:24.260 --> 01:20:27.400
that there is absence of any of these building blocks

1612
01:20:27.400 --> 01:20:30.580
that you cannot get, and therefore,

1613
01:20:30.580 --> 01:20:32.990
your body would have trouble building protein.

1614
01:20:32.990 --> 01:20:34.403
That's not the case.

1615
01:20:35.590 --> 01:20:39.060
Second thing that I think is important for all of us

1616
01:20:39.060 --> 01:20:41.720
to understand, and sometimes I overlook this

1617
01:20:41.720 --> 01:20:45.090
in my explanations, is our food contains protein,

1618
01:20:45.090 --> 01:20:47.400
whether it's chicken or whether it's beans,

1619
01:20:47.400 --> 01:20:51.330
or whether it's tofu, whatever it may be.

1620
01:20:51.330 --> 01:20:53.690
And when we digest that,

1621
01:20:53.690 --> 01:20:57.210
we break that protein down into amino acids

1622
01:20:57.210 --> 01:21:00.830
and amino acids are absorbed into the bloodstream.

1623
01:21:00.830 --> 01:21:03.850
And then once they're in our bloodstream,

1624
01:21:03.850 --> 01:21:06.700
they're not tagged, our body has no idea

1625
01:21:06.700 --> 01:21:10.960
if that was a leucine molecule from chicken,

1626
01:21:10.960 --> 01:21:12.373
or if it was from soy.

1627
01:21:13.460 --> 01:21:14.970
Once they're in the bloodstream,

1628
01:21:14.970 --> 01:21:18.340
they are just available amino acids for our body

1629
01:21:18.340 --> 01:21:22.010
to use as as it needs to, to produce protein.

1630
01:21:22.010 --> 01:21:24.870
So with all of that said,

1631
01:21:24.870 --> 01:21:28.730
then it becomes clear that we just to get enough

1632
01:21:28.730 --> 01:21:32.980
of those nine essential amino acids into our bloodstream

1633
01:21:32.980 --> 01:21:36.500
and our body will do the rest of the work.

1634
01:21:36.500 --> 01:21:39.740
The bloodstream transports them where they need to go

1635
01:21:39.740 --> 01:21:42.020
and when they arrive at their destination,

1636
01:21:42.020 --> 01:21:44.660
they're the building blocks to be assembled

1637
01:21:44.660 --> 01:21:48.300
into whatever the body needs in that specific location.

1638
01:21:48.300 --> 01:21:49.340
Exactly.

1639
01:21:49.340 --> 01:21:53.720
And I don't wanna cover, retrace too much territory,

1640
01:21:53.720 --> 01:21:56.620
but the only way that you would run into problem on

1641
01:21:56.620 --> 01:22:00.180
a plant-based diet in terms of supplying adequate amounts

1642
01:22:00.180 --> 01:22:03.040
of each of those nine essential amino acids,

1643
01:22:03.040 --> 01:22:05.770
is if you were severely under-eating

1644
01:22:05.770 --> 01:22:08.150
and getting nowhere near the total number of calories

1645
01:22:08.150 --> 01:22:12.720
that you require, or, and/or,

1646
01:22:12.720 --> 01:22:15.290
living off of one or two foods,

1647
01:22:15.290 --> 01:22:18.680
as you can often see in certain parts of the world

1648
01:22:18.680 --> 01:22:20.900
where there are food security issues.

1649
01:22:20.900 --> 01:22:22.220
And that's a whole nother situation.

1650
01:22:22.220 --> 01:22:24.510
So if you're super deficient in one of

1651
01:22:24.510 --> 01:22:26.360
the nine essential amino acids,

1652
01:22:26.360 --> 01:22:30.610
then your body can't actually rebuild those proteins

1653
01:22:30.610 --> 01:22:32.570
that it needs, and you're gonna run into problems.

1654
01:22:32.570 --> 01:22:35.080
Exactly, it becomes a limiting amino acid,

1655
01:22:35.080 --> 01:22:38.437
and then it affects protein synthesis.

1656
01:22:38.437 --> 01:22:40.363
And of course, we want to avoid that.

1657
01:22:41.680 --> 01:22:46.680
If you are eating with diversity and you're paying attention

1658
01:22:46.800 --> 01:22:50.730
to regularly consuming the protein-rich plant foods,

1659
01:22:50.730 --> 01:22:53.290
which I like to point out that most of

1660
01:22:53.290 --> 01:22:56.890
the protein is coming from the legume food group,

1661
01:22:56.890 --> 01:23:00.030
also quite a bit from nuts and seeds.

1662
01:23:00.030 --> 01:23:04.230
So if you are including regularly the likes of beans,

1663
01:23:04.230 --> 01:23:09.230
lentils, chickpeas, tofu, tempeh, legume pastas,

1664
01:23:09.550 --> 01:23:14.550
even seitan or total TVP, these are all very,

1665
01:23:16.340 --> 01:23:19.170
very protein-rich foods.

1666
01:23:19.170 --> 01:23:21.680
And as long as you're including them in your diet,

1667
01:23:21.680 --> 01:23:24.060
and I like to recommend people have

1668
01:23:24.060 --> 01:23:26.030
at least three serves of those a day,

1669
01:23:26.030 --> 01:23:28.560
a serve being about half a cup of beans

1670
01:23:28.560 --> 01:23:33.540
or half a cup of tofu, or a cup of soy milk,

1671
01:23:33.540 --> 01:23:36.113
that's another good, very protein-rich serve.

1672
01:23:37.200 --> 01:23:39.450
If as long as you are getting three of those

1673
01:23:39.450 --> 01:23:43.810
in a day across the diet, then you will be consuming

1674
01:23:43.810 --> 01:23:46.703
these essential amino acid in the required amounts.

1675
01:23:47.730 --> 01:23:49.830
Of course, if you're an athlete,

1676
01:23:49.830 --> 01:23:52.310
then you're gonna wanna pay even more attention

1677
01:23:52.310 --> 01:23:53.680
to leaning into that food group.

1678
01:23:53.680 --> 01:23:56.810
And you might consider a protein supplement,

1679
01:23:56.810 --> 01:23:59.960
which is something that all athletes consider,

1680
01:23:59.960 --> 01:24:02.310
whether they consume an omnivorous diet

1681
01:24:02.310 --> 01:24:03.990
or a plant-based diet.

1682
01:24:03.990 --> 01:24:08.284
And then there is also so a strong case to be made,

1683
01:24:08.284 --> 01:24:10.650
and I was recently speaking with Ginny Messina,

1684
01:24:10.650 --> 01:24:15.260
who's a very well-known registered dietician,

1685
01:24:15.260 --> 01:24:17.955
in the sort of plant-based community.

1686
01:24:17.955 --> 01:24:19.840
And we were talking about,

1687
01:24:19.840 --> 01:24:22.180
there is a very compelling case for paying

1688
01:24:22.180 --> 01:24:24.210
a little more attention to protein

1689
01:24:24.210 --> 01:24:26.320
as you get over the age of 50.

1690
01:24:26.320 --> 01:24:27.360
And some of that, again,

1691
01:24:27.360 --> 01:24:29.650
feeds back to our earlier conversation

1692
01:24:29.650 --> 01:24:33.863
around bone mineral density and lean muscle.

1693
01:24:34.860 --> 01:24:37.580
We know that having more lean muscle

1694
01:24:37.580 --> 01:24:40.770
as you get older is associated with longevity.

1695
01:24:40.770 --> 01:24:44.640
So, overall, as you get above 50,

1696
01:24:44.640 --> 01:24:47.010
I like people to lean a little more

1697
01:24:47.010 --> 01:24:50.750
into legumes, and to make room for that.

1698
01:24:50.750 --> 01:24:52.950
Not excluding them, but a little less

1699
01:24:52.950 --> 01:24:55.960
into whole grains and potatoes, and starches.

1700
01:24:55.960 --> 01:24:59.150
And that allows them to sort of slightly bump up

1701
01:24:59.150 --> 01:25:00.610
that protein intake.

1702
01:25:00.610 --> 01:25:03.450
And I think you've had Valter Longo

1703
01:25:03.450 --> 01:25:06.760
on here a a couple of times, and he's also an advocate

1704
01:25:06.760 --> 01:25:10.820
of that strategy, to just bump that protein up a little bit

1705
01:25:10.820 --> 01:25:15.630
to actually help increase lifespan and health outcomes.

1706
01:25:15.630 --> 01:25:17.403
So to play devil's advocate,

1707
01:25:18.250 --> 01:25:22.790
what is your response to the statement

1708
01:25:22.790 --> 01:25:26.450
that animal protein is just superior?

1709
01:25:26.450 --> 01:25:30.330
Like, is there a foundation for that argument

1710
01:25:30.330 --> 01:25:33.240
or what else goes into animal protein

1711
01:25:33.240 --> 01:25:35.850
that would provide that person

1712
01:25:35.850 --> 01:25:39.123
with some logical basis for making that statement?

1713
01:25:39.123 --> 01:25:41.870
What is superior, right?

1714
01:25:41.870 --> 01:25:42.703
Well, let's do that.

1715
01:25:42.703 --> 01:25:47.670
If we were, if you were saying to me, building lean muscle

1716
01:25:50.070 --> 01:25:52.350
is it easier to build lean muscle with

1717
01:25:52.350 --> 01:25:55.510
a diet full of animal products than plant-based products

1718
01:25:55.510 --> 01:25:57.550
with less consideration and planning?

1719
01:25:57.550 --> 01:25:59.120
Yes, it is.

1720
01:25:59.120 --> 01:26:01.890
I mean, we have to be able to acknowledge that.

1721
01:26:01.890 --> 01:26:04.560
That's not saying you can't get the same result,

1722
01:26:04.560 --> 01:26:07.020
you can, but you have to plan a little more

1723
01:26:07.020 --> 01:26:09.030
on the plant-based side of things.

1724
01:26:09.030 --> 01:26:13.640
But my definition of superior is I want be fit

1725
01:26:13.640 --> 01:26:15.620
and I wanna build muscle, but at the same time

1726
01:26:15.620 --> 01:26:17.617
I want consider my long-term health outcomes,

1727
01:26:17.617 --> 01:26:19.920
and I want to consider longevity.

1728
01:26:19.920 --> 01:26:24.920
And we have to understand that there's a trade off here.

1729
01:26:26.010 --> 01:26:30.500
One of the big differences is the ratio

1730
01:26:30.500 --> 01:26:32.700
and concentration of particular amino acids

1731
01:26:32.700 --> 01:26:35.090
between animal and plant foods.

1732
01:26:35.090 --> 01:26:40.090
And while animal foods might be very rich

1733
01:26:40.670 --> 01:26:43.610
in certain amino acids that activate mTOR

1734
01:26:43.610 --> 01:26:45.830
for example, and raise IGF-1 levels,

1735
01:26:45.830 --> 01:26:49.500
and while that might be great for promoting growth,

1736
01:26:49.500 --> 01:26:53.300
we understand through a lot of mechanistic research,

1737
01:26:53.300 --> 01:26:56.680
and I will preface this by saying most of this is

1738
01:26:56.680 --> 01:26:59.580
in animal models because it cannot be done in humans.

1739
01:26:59.580 --> 01:27:01.270
At least not at this stage.

1740
01:27:01.270 --> 01:27:04.360
We understand that over-activating those,

1741
01:27:04.360 --> 01:27:08.440
works against you in terms of your longevity.

1742
01:27:08.440 --> 01:27:13.440
So, this is not a sort of simple answer

1743
01:27:13.680 --> 01:27:15.600
or simple question of what's superior.

1744
01:27:15.600 --> 01:27:18.450
It's really, what's meaningful to you.

1745
01:27:18.450 --> 01:27:22.080
And, personally, I made the decision

1746
01:27:22.080 --> 01:27:27.080
that I want to be consuming foods that are allowing me

1747
01:27:27.760 --> 01:27:31.290
to achieve my athletic endeavors,

1748
01:27:31.290 --> 01:27:34.563
pursue those goals, but at the same time,

1749
01:27:35.610 --> 01:27:40.390
not over activate mTOR to the point that I'm putting myself

1750
01:27:40.390 --> 01:27:42.100
at higher risk of certain diseases

1751
01:27:42.100 --> 01:27:45.430
and potentially shortening my overall lifespan.

1752
01:27:45.430 --> 01:27:48.140
So if you're 24 years old and all you care

1753
01:27:48.140 --> 01:27:51.520
about is how you're gonna perform in four months

1754
01:27:51.520 --> 01:27:54.130
at the championships or what have you,

1755
01:27:54.130 --> 01:27:56.040
and you don't care about anything else,

1756
01:27:56.040 --> 01:27:58.750
all you want to do is ramp up that IGF-1

1757
01:27:58.750 --> 01:28:02.210
and get as much strength and growth as conceivable

1758
01:28:02.210 --> 01:28:03.830
in a short period of time.

1759
01:28:03.830 --> 01:28:08.450
But that's at the cost of longevity, longterm health,

1760
01:28:08.450 --> 01:28:11.500
all these things that are important

1761
01:28:11.500 --> 01:28:15.350
in terms of maintaining vitality, fitness, strength,

1762
01:28:15.350 --> 01:28:19.770
all of those things over the extension of your lifetime.

1763
01:28:19.770 --> 01:28:24.770
And even as a kind of a plant-based person,

1764
01:28:25.410 --> 01:28:28.490
you can still modulate your diet

1765
01:28:28.490 --> 01:28:32.010
to promote muscle growth and strength.

1766
01:28:32.010 --> 01:28:33.940
And I spoke to one of the studies that looked

1767
01:28:33.940 --> 01:28:38.940
at comparing healthy young adults,

1768
01:28:40.440 --> 01:28:42.120
omnivorous diet versus a vegan diet,

1769
01:28:42.120 --> 01:28:44.410
and they matched that total protein intake

1770
01:28:44.410 --> 01:28:47.640
at about 1.6, 1.7 grams per kilo,

1771
01:28:47.640 --> 01:28:49.793
which is a high protein intake.

1772
01:28:50.880 --> 01:28:54.370
And they saw no differences in lean muscle and strength.

1773
01:28:54.370 --> 01:28:55.780
That's really important point to make.

1774
01:28:55.780 --> 01:29:00.780
So I think that there is a way to kind of have both.

1775
01:29:00.890 --> 01:29:05.890
And so you can shift to a plant-based diet

1776
01:29:07.720 --> 01:29:11.120
and then modify it in a way where you are getting

1777
01:29:11.120 --> 01:29:15.620
the performance gains that you are looking for.

1778
01:29:15.620 --> 01:29:18.540
And I still think you are better off, better protected

1779
01:29:18.540 --> 01:29:20.660
than the animal-based diet.

1780
01:29:20.660 --> 01:29:23.010
Are you potentially making a trade off compared

1781
01:29:23.010 --> 01:29:26.220
to a slightly lower protein plant-based diet?

1782
01:29:26.220 --> 01:29:28.350
I think, possibly.

1783
01:29:28.350 --> 01:29:31.440
And that's based on those mechanistic animal studies,

1784
01:29:31.440 --> 01:29:34.800
but back to your earlier point, people have different goals.

1785
01:29:34.800 --> 01:29:37.780
And for some people, it will be meaningful

1786
01:29:37.780 --> 01:29:42.200
to build muscle and to build strength

1787
01:29:43.330 --> 01:29:45.670
while adopting this dietary pattern

1788
01:29:45.670 --> 01:29:48.420
that is overall better for their chronic disease,

1789
01:29:48.420 --> 01:29:50.870
better for the planet, and better for the animals.

1790
01:29:50.870 --> 01:29:53.480
In addition, if we're gonna contemplate

1791
01:29:53.480 --> 01:29:55.520
like quote unquote "gains,"

1792
01:29:55.520 --> 01:29:58.070
you also have to consider all of these other things,

1793
01:29:58.070 --> 01:30:02.063
like inflammation and foods that you're are eating

1794
01:30:02.063 --> 01:30:05.010
that are gonna allow your body to recover more quickly.

1795
01:30:05.010 --> 01:30:07.920
And if you're eating a predominantly plant-based diet,

1796
01:30:07.920 --> 01:30:11.340
chances are, it's gonna be more anti-inflammatory

1797
01:30:11.340 --> 01:30:14.440
than an animal-based or animal-centric diet,

1798
01:30:14.440 --> 01:30:17.290
which is gonna allow your body to recover more quickly.

1799
01:30:17.290 --> 01:30:19.800
So that has to go into that equation as well.

1800
01:30:19.800 --> 01:30:22.610
And I think there is something in that

1801
01:30:22.610 --> 01:30:24.800
that we haven't yet determined from science,

1802
01:30:24.800 --> 01:30:27.480
but there's a lot of anecdotal evidence

1803
01:30:27.480 --> 01:30:29.670
from athletes toward the end of their careers

1804
01:30:29.670 --> 01:30:32.030
who are shifting towards more plant-based.

1805
01:30:32.030 --> 01:30:36.120
And really cool, and I wish I had details to share,

1806
01:30:36.120 --> 01:30:38.560
and it'll sound like I'm obsessed with Stanford,

1807
01:30:38.560 --> 01:30:41.350
but hey, they're doing great research.

1808
01:30:41.350 --> 01:30:42.590
